# .gitleaksignore -- historical findings gitleaks must not re-report.
#
# Every entry is COMMIT-scoped (`<40-hex commit>:<file>:<rule>:<line>`), so it
# silences that one historical commit and nothing a later commit adds. A
# file-scoped entry (`<file>:<rule>:<line>`) would also hide any FUTURE secret
# written at that line, so none is allowed here, and every entry sits under a
# comment naming its class (juniper_data/tests/unit/test_gitleaks_config.py
# enforces both).
#
# Only push / pull_request scans are commit-ranged; a workflow_dispatch or
# schedule run of ci.yml scans every commit reachable from every remote branch
# and tag. These entries are what that scan finds once .gitleaks.toml loads the
# default ruleset, measured 2026-10-25 with gitleaks 8.24.3 (juniper-ml's
# util/ad-hoc/2026-10-10_gitleaks_probe.py `scan --scope ci-dispatch`, driven by
# util/ad-hoc/2026-10-25_gitleaks_probe_for_repo.py: 90 remote head/tag SHAs,
# 1140 commits, 27 findings) and classified with
# util/ad-hoc/2026-10-10_gitleaks_finding_inspect.py (shape and masked context
# only; no value was printed). Values are never written here.
#
# The config's own [allowlist].commits entry (4bd37a6c, the ARC-AGI identifier)
# is a separate, older decision and is not repeated here. Conda build strings in
# the conda environment files are also covered by the AND-scoped generic-api-key
# allowlist in .gitleaks.toml, which, unlike an entry here, holds for commits
# that do not exist yet; the Hash entries below are kept as the record of the
# historical findings.
#
